<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>


<worksheet xml:id="ws-polynomial-rings-activity">

	<title>Module 11: Polynomial Rings &amp; Divisibility</title>
	<introduction><p><alert>The X-Factor</alert></p></introduction>
	
	<page>

	<paragraphs>
		<title>Are polynomials just like integers?</title>
		
    <p>
       There's a bunch of things we could do in the integers that it turns out we can also do with polynomials. Here's a table to compare/contrast. (Note here that <m>R</m> is a commutative ring.)
    </p>
    
    <p><image source="images/Polynomials-Properties.png" width="95%" /></p>
    </paragraphs>
    </page>


	<page>
	
	<paragraphs>
		<title>Divides</title>
    
    	<exercise workspace="0.5in">
			<p>Using your knowledge of divides in the integers, state a definition of divides in the polynomial ring <m>R[x]</m> where <m>R</m> is a commutative ring.</p>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise workspace="1.5in" xml:id="poly-divides-ex-1">
			<p>
			Does <m>a(x)=x^2+2</m> divide <m>b(x)=x^5+7x^3+x^2+10x+2</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}[x]</m>? Use polynomial long division to determine.
			</p>
		</exercise>
    
    	<exercise workspace="1.5in" xml:id="poly-divides-ex-2">
			<p>
			Does <m>a(x)=x+2</m> divide <m>b(x)=3x^4 -5x^2 +3</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}[x]</m>? Use polynomial long division to determine.
			</p>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise workspace="1in">
			<p>
			(If time:) Prove or disprove:
			</p> 
			<p>For a field <m>F</m> and <m>f(x),g(x)\in F[x]</m>, if <m>f(x)</m> divides <m>g(x)</m> and <m>c\in F</m>, <m>c\neq 0</m>, then <m>cf(x)</m> divides <m>g(x)</m>.
			</p>
		</exercise>
    </paragraphs>

	</page>
	
	<page>
	<paragraphs>
		<title>The Division Algorithm in <m>F[x]</m></title>
		
		<exercise workspace="0.5in">
		<p>
    We restrict talking about the division algorithm to <m>F[x]</m> where
    <m>F</m> is a field.
    When <m>F</m> is a field, <m>F[x]</m> is an integral domain.
    Why is <m>F[x]</m> never a field?
		</p>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise workspace="0.5in">
		<p>
    Why is <m>\mathbb{Z}_{4}[x]</m> not an integral domain?
		</p>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise workspace="0.5in">
		<p>
    Why does <m>F[x]</m> for a field <m>F</m> not have zero divisors?
    (Hint: Consider the degree of the product of two polynomials.)
		</p>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise workspace="0.5in">
		<p>
    Use <xref ref="poly-divides-ex-1" /> and <xref ref="poly-divides-ex-2" /> from the previous page to write statements that <q>look like</q>
    the division algorithm for integers (e.g., <m>b=aq+r</m>, where
    <m>a = a(x)</m> and <m>b=b(x)</m>, what are <m>q(x)</m> and <m>r(x)</m>?).
		</p>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise workspace="0.5in">
		<p>
    In the division algorithm we had a restriction on the remainder <m>r</m>:
    <m>0\le r &lt; a</m>.
    Based on your answer to the previous question, what would be a reasonable
    restriction on the remainder in the division algorithm in polynomials?
		</p>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise workspace="0.5in">
		<p>
    The Division Algorithm for polynomials says: Let <m>F</m> be a field and let
    <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> be polynomials in <m>F[x]</m> with
    <m>g(x)\neq 0</m>.
    Then there exist unique polynomials <m>q(x)</m> and <m>r(x)</m> in
    <m>F[x]</m> such that
    <md>
        f(x)=g(x)q(x)+r(x)
    </md>
    where <m>r(x)=0</m> or <m>\deg(r(x)) &lt; \deg(g(x))</m>.
		</p>

		<p>
    What are the similarities and differences with the division algorithm for
    integers?
		</p>
		</exercise>
		
	</paragraphs>
	</page>
	
	<page>
	<paragraphs>
	<title>Greatest Common Divisor</title>
	
	<exercise workspace="1in">
	<p>
    Suppose in <m>\mathbb{R}[x]</m> we have <m>f(x)=2x^{2}+x-6</m> and
    <m>g(x)=2x^{2}-7x+6</m>.
    Note that we have the following
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>f(x)=(x+2)(2x-3)</m> and <m>g(x)=(x-2)(2x-3)</m>
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                <m>f(x)=(2x+4)(x-\frac{3}{2})</m> and
                <m>g(x) = (2x-4)(x-\frac{3}{2})</m>
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                <m>f(x) = \left(\frac{1}{2}x+1)(4x-6)\right)</m> and
                <m>g(x)=\left(\frac{1}{2}x-1\right)(4x-6)</m>
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
	</p>

	<p>
    Based on this, what are some common divisors of <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m>?
	</p>
	</exercise>
	
	<exercise workspace="1in">
	<p>
    If we want the greatest common divisor to be unique, which common divisor
    should we pick?
	</p>
	</exercise>
	
	<exercise workspace="1in">
	<p>
    Here's a definition:</p>
    <definition>
    	<statement>Let <m>F</m> be a field and let <m>f(x), g(x)\in F[x]</m>
    that are not both zero.
    The <term>greatest common divisor</term> of <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> is
    the polynomial <m>d(x)\in F[x]</m> that satisfies the following three
    conditions:
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                <m>d(x)</m> divides <m>f(x)</m> and <m>d(x)</m> divides
                <m>g(x)</m>
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                If <m>h(x)\in F[x]</m> and <m>h(x)</m> divides both <m>f(x)</m>
                and <m>g(x)</m> then <m>h(x)</m> divides <m>d(x)</m>
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <p>
                <m>d(x)</m> is a monic polynomial (i.e., the leading coefficient
                is <m>1_{F}</m> )
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
    	</statement>
	</definition>
    <p>What are the similarities and differences between this definition and the
    integer definition?
	</p>
	</exercise>
	
	</paragraphs>
	</page>

	<page>
	<paragraphs>
	<title>Relatively Prime and Euclidean Algorithm</title>
	
	<exercise workspace="1in">
	 <p>
     Let <m>F</m> be a field.
     Fill in the definition: Two polynomials <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> in
     <m>F[x]</m> are <term>relatively prime</term> provided that...
	 </p>
	</exercise>
	
	 <exercise workspace="6in">
	 <p>
     Just like in the integers, one can repeatedly apply the division algorithm
     to find the greatest common divisor of two polynomials.
     Apply the Euclidean algorithm to determien <m>\gcd(f(x), g(x))</m> for
     <m>f(x)=3x^{5}+7x^{4}+11x^{3}+15x^{2}+10x+2</m> and
     <m>g(x) = 3x^{2}+7x+2</m>.
	 </p>
	</exercise>
	</paragraphs>
	</page>
	

<page xml:id="sec-polynomial-rings-review">

<paragraphs>
<title>Review questions</title>
Make sure you can answer the following questions:

<ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            What does it mean for a polynomial to divide another polynomial?
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            If <m>F</m> is a field, what's the strongest thing you can say about
            <m>F[x]</m>?
            If <m>R</m> is a commutative ring, what's the strongest thing you
            can say about <m>R[x]</m>?
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            What does the division algorithm say for <m>F[x]</m> where <m>F</m>
            is a field?
            What are the similarities/differences with the integer division
            algorithm?
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            What is the definition for greatest common divisor of <m>f(x)</m>
            and <m>g(x)</m> in <m>F[x]</m>?
            What are the similarities/differences with the integers?
        </p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>
            What does it mean for two polynomials to be relatively prime?
            How can you apply the Euclidean algorithm in <m>F[x]</m>?
        </p>
    </li>
</ul>
                
<p>The rest of this page is for you to summarize important ideas from the module.</p>

</paragraphs>

</page>


<page>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Weekly Practice 11</title>

		<exercise number="1">
		<statement><p>Fill in the table on p. 1 of this module with careful definitions of each.
		</p></statement>
    	</exercise>
	
	
		<exercise number="2">
		<title>Find a polynomial GCD</title>
		<statement><p>
    In <m>\mathbb{Z}_{3}[x]</m>, let <m>f(x)=x^{4}+[2]x^{3}+x+[2]</m> and
    <m>g(x)=[2]x^{2}+[1]</m>.
    Find <m>\gcd(f(x),g(x))</m> using the Euclidean algorithm.
		</p></statement>
		</exercise>
		
		<exercise number="3">
		<title>Factoring polynomials</title>
		<introduction><p>
    In this module we've explored how polynomials are like integers.
    We've explored factoring polynomials a bit (and you likely explored it a lot
    in high school).
    Let's explore some more.
		</p></introduction>
        	<task><p>
    Let <m>f(x)=x^{2}-3x-2</m> be in <m>\mathbb{R}[x]</m>.
    Notice that <m>f(5)=8</m>.
    What is the remainder when <m>f(x)</m> is divided by <m>x-5</m>?
			</p></task>
            <task><p>
    Let <m>g(x) = x^{2}-3x-10</m> be in <m>\mathbb{R}[x]</m>.
    Determine <m>g(5)</m> and determine the remainder when <m>g(x)</m> is
    divided by <m>x-5</m>.
			</p></task>
			<task><p>
    Let <m>h(x)=x^{2}-3x-10</m> be in <m>\mathbb{Q}[x]</m>.
    Determine <m>h(-2)</m> and determine the remainder when <m>h(x)</m> is
    divided by <m>x-(-2)</m>.
			</p></task>
		</exercise>
	
	</paragraphs>
	
 </page>
 
 </worksheet>

